f r(t)=\\langle t,2 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(2 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(t)(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{\\sqrt{5} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left((3)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left(9-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542603 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542607 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x \\left(y - 1\\right)$ along the line $y=2 x + 1,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542607 1072 #uuid "2bf9d63c-b3f4-5d38-83c5-c18e8598dd9c" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542607 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2 t + 1.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(2 t + 1)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x \\left(y - 1\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}(2 t^{2})(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5} t^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\left((2)^{3}-(0)^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\left(8-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542598 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542608 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=5 x$ along the line $y=6 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 7.$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542608 1072 #uuid "2eade040-7c90-5638-966c-718b41cfb998" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542608 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=6 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,6 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 7\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(6 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(6)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(6)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{37}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=5 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{7}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{7}(5 t)(\\sqrt{37})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{7} \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37}}{2}\\left((7)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37}}{2}\\left(49-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542600 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542609 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=3 x$ along the line $y=4 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 5.$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542609 1072 #uuid "6ab95a8c-ac51-5396-919b-6a451ca13ac6" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542609 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=4 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,4 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 5\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(4 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(4)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(4)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{5}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{5}(3 t)(\\sqrt{17})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{2}\\left((5)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{2}\\left(25-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542604 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542610 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)$ along the line $y=3x+2,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542610 1072 #uuid "74138378-8762-53b8-ac46-3f0ace177732" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542610 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,3t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)j \\\\  & =i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{2}+(3t+2)={t}^{2}+3t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\,(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+2t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{8}{3}+6+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542602 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542611 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$ along the curve $y={e}^{x},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}.$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542611 1072 #uuid "7e94a1c8-32a5-5aeb-82a3-988f3971786f" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542611 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{17}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({17}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(17 \\sqrt{17}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542599 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542612 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=4 x$ along the line $y=5 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 6.$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542612 1072 #uuid "969d5e52-a658-5dd3-9654-fc884ce1b67b" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542612 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=5 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,5 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(5 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(5)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(5)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=4 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{6}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{6}(4 t)(\\sqrt{26})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[2 \\sqrt{26} t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{6} \\\\ &=2 \\sqrt{26}\\left((6)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=2 \\sqrt{26}\\left(36-0\\right) \\\\ &=72 \\sqrt{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542601 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542613 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2 x$ along the line $y=3 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4.$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542613 1072 #uuid "fa2eae58-23f0-53d8-954b-616c559e82a9" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true] [17592186542613 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,3 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(3 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(3)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{4}(2 t)(\\sqrt{10})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\sqrt{10} t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{4} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\left((4)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\left(16-0\\right) \\\\ &=16 \\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535924 17592186045443 true]] :edb/tempids {"a-020d5907-9b7b-5719-a1f1-4f44c5409284" 17592186542557 "a-07329d51-3f5e-5e2e-8441-b2141aff317e" 